{"access":"public","aliases":[],"assertions":[{"claim_ids":["clm_1f2462dd33c2d7b3568bf098048a28a547535e0edbf742c37435caba85605166"],"opposing_claim_ids":[],"text":"A shared HttpClientHelper component performs the actual HTTP send, and its DefaultJsonSerializerOptions singleton can be changed; by default PropertyNameCaseInsensitive is true."},{"claim_ids":["clm_2082704fcac7ad0b1684e0868dd3273b36da797da4d0880a7caded6e1ced4fe2"],"opposing_claim_ids":[],"text":"The generator emits a constructor taking an HttpClient parameter, and the generated method builds route, query, and header dictionaries before delegating to HttpClientHelper.SendAsync."},{"claim_ids":["clm_304fb050a1c5157ae6855b48ca14c512fb94e64a3a0222f1ab46cb05f33d901a"],"opposing_claim_ids":[],"text":"If the user defines their own HttpClient field/property or a parameterless method returning HttpClient, the generator will use that instead of injecting via constructor."},{"claim_ids":["clm_47d345402434dc7e9afa6b4eeefbc05dc2b9af148658a56d189f1d3bfd7f60dc"],"opposing_claim_ids":[],"text":"The package targets .NET 5 console apps and is installed via the HttpClientGenerator NuGet package; generated code relies on HttpClientGenerator.Shared helpers and System.Text.Json serialization attributes."},{"claim_ids":["clm_4a4491cd845c62b1d2f98860ea5f7b28ae3eda59e758a161ee5c17258813e5ab"],"opposing_claim_ids":[],"text":"The tool uses the Roslyn C# source generator feature to write boilerplate HttpClient code on behalf of the developer."},{"claim_ids":["clm_57d947abd23d4c31c5edd3633f9650edf8dde944dc0c2045380a69990c3e3bda"],"opposing_claim_ids":[],"text":"Generated code is produced in-memory at build time and never written to disk, so it needs no source-control tracking."},{"claim_ids":["clm_5c5fbd0bc953a26b6aec7f2bd6e3ddcb7645257fe3bae051739cab4edfec1230"],"opposing_claim_ids":[],"text":"The tool appears to support route parameters by substituting method arguments into the attribute's path template, as shown by the id route and dictionary usage."},{"claim_ids":["clm_80149ea18ecfd4bc42896614332984f8b60681b9ff13dc5f9c33ee67657b6d0e"],"opposing_claim_ids":[],"text":"The README strongly recommends using IHttpClientFactory to resolve HttpClient instances rather than manual instantiation."},{"claim_ids":["clm_99c1f787e911def18f47be0cab3b3f742ace2fa8b6102071d58649eb559d2c0d"],"opposing_claim_ids":[],"text":"The design philosophy is that no boilerplate or auto-generated code should be written, tracked in the repository, or regenerated manually when HTTP contracts change."},{"claim_ids":["clm_a6f2e9847f5bc33b0f313163c547309f7bacd19d38372841446349cfa64022be"],"opposing_claim_ids":[],"text":"Known issues include needing a Visual Studio 2019 restart for IntelliSense, a referenced Roslyn issue during development, and incomplete OmniSharp support in VS Code, though the dotnet SDK works."},{"claim_ids":["clm_b1337f2071efa93a672eebdb588d140bab7f081f9e6148fc8d84e56da3ff0e8b"],"opposing_claim_ids":[],"text":"Users declare partial classes with partial methods decorated by attributes such as HttpGet containing route templates like \"todos/{id}\"; the generator implements those methods."},{"claim_ids":["clm_cbeeeb77440fe920478a2afb85a2f0b101b880a0f5a7cf81db577e1bd216e14a"],"opposing_claim_ids":[],"text":"The library claims no runtime dependency on third-party code, since generation happens at compile time within the consumer's project."}],"claim_ids":["clm_1f2462dd33c2d7b3568bf098048a28a547535e0edbf742c37435caba85605166","clm_2082704fcac7ad0b1684e0868dd3273b36da797da4d0880a7caded6e1ced4fe2","clm_304fb050a1c5157ae6855b48ca14c512fb94e64a3a0222f1ab46cb05f33d901a","clm_47d345402434dc7e9afa6b4eeefbc05dc2b9af148658a56d189f1d3bfd7f60dc","clm_4a4491cd845c62b1d2f98860ea5f7b28ae3eda59e758a161ee5c17258813e5ab","clm_57d947abd23d4c31c5edd3633f9650edf8dde944dc0c2045380a69990c3e3bda","clm_5c5fbd0bc953a26b6aec7f2bd6e3ddcb7645257fe3bae051739cab4edfec1230","clm_80149ea18ecfd4bc42896614332984f8b60681b9ff13dc5f9c33ee67657b6d0e